# Join the CivOS experiment

CivOS is testing whether a structured decision trail helps a small group recover its reasons, preserve objections, and correct mistakes with less effort than its existing process. The repository contains a local Python prototype and a proposed pilot method. There are no published pilot results here.

A useful contribution makes one claim more precise, one failure easier to reproduce, or one decision easier to review.

## Five bounded contributions

1. **Reconstruct a decision.** Run the synthetic demo, read its report, and identify the claim, supporting and challenging evidence, responsible decision-maker, and unresolved questions. Report what you could not find without reading the code.
2. **Break a record assumption.** Create a minimal synthetic input that should be rejected, or a valid input the application mishandles. Include the command, observed output, and expected behavior.
3. **Test disagreement.** Describe a case where two reasonable assessments conflict. Show how the record should preserve that conflict when somebody still has to decide.
4. **Design a comparison.** Propose one low-stakes task, the group's existing notes or spreadsheet as a baseline, and a practical measurement of accuracy, retrieval time, and recording burden. Define in advance what result would make you abandon the proposed workflow.
5. **Repair a source claim.** Identify a statement in the documentation that a cited source does not support. Supply the exact claim, the relevant passage or section, and a narrower replacement where appropriate.

No technical contribution is required to identify a bad assumption. A maintenance practitioner can explain why a reported outcome would be meaningless. A researcher can challenge the comparison method. A participant can show that the record misrepresents their objection. Each is a concrete design contribution.

## What a pilot proposal needs

State the decision, its actual decision-maker, affected participants, available authority, and the limits of the experiment. Explain what information must remain outside the ledger, how participants can challenge their representation, and who will perform the review.

Choose a reversible activity with consequences small enough to understand. Use synthetic cases first. The current software has no authentication, access control, consent workflow, or selective erasure process, so an initial proposal should not depend on collecting sensitive material.

A proposal should end with a stop condition. If an ordinary document performs as well, that is useful evidence. If the system makes objections harder to see or responsibility harder to identify, the design needs to change.

## Where to start

Use the [repository](https://github.com/krisledel/civos) for source and contributions. Read the [README](README.md), [contribution guide](CONTRIBUTING.md), and [threat model](thedarkmirror). Open a focused issue or submit a pull request with a small, reviewable change.

The project does not currently document a separate forum, elected council, regular meeting programme, or confidential reporting service. The repository is the reference point for its published work and current procedures.
